A small hint to start: try not to count subarrays directly. Think in terms of running totals (prefix sums) and how often a particular total has appeared before. Does that nudge you toward a cleaner solution?

Idea (sketch)
- Let prefix[i] be the sum of nums[0..i-1]. Then the sum of a subarray nums[i..j] equals prefix[j+1] - prefix[i].
- We want prefix[j+1] - prefix[i] == k, i.e. prefix[i] == prefix[j+1] - k.
- As we scan the array and maintain the current running sum S = prefix[j+1], we only need to know how many previous prefix values equal S - k. Keep a frequency map of prefix sums seen so far.
- Initialize freq[0] = 1 (empty prefix) so subarrays starting at index 0 are counted.
- For each element, update S, add freq[S - k] (if any) to the answer, then increment freq[S].

Complexity
- Time: O(n) — each element processed once.
- Space: O(n) in worst case for distinct prefix sums.
- Handles negative numbers, zeros, and any k because it doesn’t rely on monotonicity.

A couple of subtle notes
- Use a 64-bit running sum to be safe (values and k can be negative, length up to 2e4 and values up to ±1000).
- The final count fits in a 32-bit signed int for these constraints, but using a 64-bit counter avoids surprises.

If this sounds good, here’s a simple, practical C++ implementation you can compile and run. You might adapt the main() to your input style if you prefer.

C++ code

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

long long subarraySum(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<long long, int> freq;
    freq.reserve(nums.size() * 2);
    freq.max_load_factor(0.7f);

    long long running = 0;
    long long count = 0;
    freq[0] = 1; // empty prefix

    for (int num : nums) {
        running += num;
        long long need = running - k;
        auto it = freq.find(need);
        if (it != freq.end()) count += it->second;
        ++freq[running];
    }
    return count;
}

int main() {
    // Example 1
    vector<int> a1 = {1,1,1};
    int k1 = 2;
    cout << "Example 1 -> " << subarraySum(a1, k1) << "\n"; // expected 2

    // Example 2
    vector<int> a2 = {1,2,3};
    int k2 = 3;
    cout << "Example 2 -> " << subarraySum(a2, k2) << "\n"; // expected 2

    // Replace the above with any input you like.
    return 0;
}

