A quiet suggestion: use the C++ <random> facilities — they do the “even distribution” work for you. Below is a lean, reliable pattern; you can tweak seeding depending on whether you want reproducible output or true nondeterminism.

Simple program (uniform 0–19, inclusive):

#include <iostream>
#include <random>

int main() {
    std::random_device rd;            // non-deterministic seed if available
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());           // mersenne twister engine
    std::uniform_int_distribution<int> dist(0, 19); // inclusive range

    int n = dist(gen);
    std::cout << n << '\n';
    return 0;
}

